---
import TechniquePost from "../../layouts/TechniquePost.astro"
import FluidTypeBuild from "../../components/UnderTheHood/FluidTypeBuild.astro"
import Code from "../../components/Code.astro"
---

<TechniquePost slug="fluid-type-progress" topic="fluid">
  <section>
    <h2 id="the-problem">The problem</h2>
    <p>
      Fluid type used to be a <code>clamp()</code> per heading: a minimum, a slope
      in <code>vi</code>, a maximum. The slope decides where the heading starts
      and stops growing, and each heading ends up with its own range.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>clamp(2rem, 9vi, 3.5rem)</code> grows between 356px and 622px
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>clamp(1.1rem, 3vi, 1.25rem)</code> grows between 587px and 667px
      </li>
      <li>
        At 600px the <code>h1</code> is almost full size while the <code
          >h4</code
        >
        has barely started, so the scale compresses
      </li>
      <li>
        Move a step of the type scale and the range moves with it, without
        anyone asking for new breakpoints
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="the-trick">The trick</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>progress(value, start, end)</code> returns where <code>value</code
        >
        sits between <code>start</code> and <code>end</code>: 0 at the start, 1
        at the end, clamped in between
      </li>
      <li>
        With <code>100vi</code> as the value, one window of viewport widths becomes
        one ramp, computed once on <code>html</code>
      </li>
      <li>
        Each heading mixes its two steps with the ramp: <code
          >min + (max - min) * progress</code
        >
      </li>
      <li>
        The window is two tokens, so a theme moves the breakpoints without
        touching a slope, and a step change keeps them where they are
      </li>
      <li>
        Any other length can ride the same ramp, like a spacing scale that
        tightens on narrow screens
      </li>
    </ul>
    <Code
      lang="css"
      code={`:where(html) {
  --ui-fluid-min-inline-size: 20rem;
  --ui-fluid-max-inline-size: 48rem;
}

:where(html, .ui-palette, .ui-contrast-more) {
  --ui-fluid-progress: progress(
    100vi,
    var(--ui-fluid-min-inline-size),
    var(--ui-fluid-max-inline-size)
  );
  --ui-font-size-h1: calc(
    var(--ui-font-size-700) +
      (var(--ui-font-size-900) - var(--ui-font-size-700)) *
      var(--ui-fluid-progress)
  );
}

:where(html) {
  --ui-space-scale: calc(0.875 + 0.125 * var(--ui-fluid-progress));
}`}
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="try-it">Try it</h2>
    <FluidTypeBuild />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="fallback">Fallback</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>progress()</code> is Baseline newly available since September 2026:
        Chrome 138, Safari 26 and Firefox 155
      </li>
      <li>
        The arguments are separated by commas, like <code>clamp()</code>. An
        earlier draft wrote <code>progress(value from start to end)</code>, and
        that form parses nowhere
      </li>
      <li>
        The theme keeps the <code>clamp()</code> sizes in <code
          >@supports not (font-size: calc(1rem * progress(1, 0, 1)))</code
        >, so an older browser gets the old slopes instead of headings that fall
        back to the body size
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>--ui-fluid-progress</code> has no fallback of its own. In an older browser
        a declaration that reads it is invalid at computed-value time, and the property
        takes its inherited or initial value
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</TechniquePost>
